items.q:
6. Identify the bug and fix it:
int i = 1;
while (i < 10);
{
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
}

items.a:
Bug: There is a semicolon after while(i < 10). This creates an empty loop body — while(i < 10); loops forever doing nothing (infinite loop) because i is never updated. The { } block below is just a regular code block, not the loop body. It would never be reached because the while loop above never exits. Fix: Remove the semicolon:
int i = 1;
while (i < 10) {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
}
This is one of the most sneaky typo bugs in Java — the semicolon is valid syntax (empty statement) so the compiler doesn't report an error.

int row = 1;
while (row <= 5) {
    int col = 1;
    while (col <= row) {
        System.out.print(col + " ");
        col++;
    }
    System.out.println();  // New line after each row
    row++;
}
// Outer loop controls rows (1 to 5).
// Inner loop prints columns from 1 up to the current row number.
// Key: inner loop condition is col <= row (not col <= 5).
// For each outer iteration, col is reset to 1.

